INT. COIN LAUNDRY, ALL-NIGHT - NIGHT

Dryers turning. Fluorescent hum. Orange plastic chairs bolted in rows. A vending machine sells soap and nothing else.

DET. SOLEDAD "SOL" MARCHETTI (40s, windbreaker, paper cup) takes the chair nearest the door. Opposite her, ARCHIE PENNYWHISTLE (60s, flannel, crossword) folds towels with a care that borders on ceremony. Near the dryers, RENATA GÓMEZ-LUND (30s, scrubs, earbuds) watches a spinning load of white shirts.

Marchetti sips. Looks at no one in particular.

MARCHETTI
Slow night.

ARCHIE
They all are, now.

He sets a towel on the pile. Squares the corners.

MARCHETTI
You work here long?

ARCHIE
Eleven years. Owner's my cousin. He doesn't come in.

MARCHETTI
Nice. A quiet job.

ARCHIE
It is until it isn't.

Renata pulls out an earbud. Glances at Marchetti. Looks away. Her load stops. She opens the door and pulls the shirts into a basket, one by one. Each is folded before it is warm.

MARCHETTI
Those look like hospital shirts.

RENATA
They're mine.

MARCHETTI
All of them?

RENATA
It's a big family.

Archie turns the page of his crossword. Pencils in a word. Erases it.

MARCHETTI
Seven letters. "Alibi."

ARCHIE
Excuse?

MARCHETTI
Six. Excuse is six.

ARCHIE
(not looking up)
Then I don't have one.

The bell on the door. TOMASZ BRZOZOWSKI (30s, leather jacket, wet hair) enters with a duffel and stops. His eyes go to Renata. Then to Marchetti's windbreaker, to the badge clipped beneath it. He nods once, pleasantly, like a man at the wrong party.

TOMASZ
Evening.

MARCHETTI
Evening.

ARCHIE
Machine three is broken, friend. Use five.

TOMASZ
I've got my own, thanks.

He unzips the duffel. Takes out a single folded shirt. Holds it up to the light. Renata drops the basket.

Nobody speaks. A dryer thuds, thuds, thuds. A coin rattles down inside it.

ARCHIE
Machine five. It's good.

MARCHETTI
^Mr. Brzozowski. Sit down.

Tomasz folds the shirt into quarters. Then smaller. A kind of smile.

TOMASZ
I haven't given my name.

MARCHETTI
You didn't need to.

From the back, a door opens. WINSTON "WIN" ABARA (50s, owner's cousin's lawyer, expensive coat) steps out of the supply closet, buttoning his jacket. He looks at the room. Nobody looks at him.

WIN
It's all right, Tomasz. They only have the one.

TOMASZ
The one what?

WIN
(gently)
The one thing, son. The rest is dryer lint.

Marchetti stands. Her cup is empty. She sets it very neatly on the chair.

MARCHETTI
Then let's start with the one.

Renata picks up the basket again. Archie closes his crossword. The dryers keep turning.
